    <!-- ── Opening Finding ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="weekly-section weekly-section--lead">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="weekly-secnum">Opening finding</div>
        <h2>Coding agents at 300+ organizations published internal screenshots to public GitHub repositories without approval</h2>
        <p class="weekly-lead-p">Glow Labs reported on 29 September that developers' AI coding agents published more than 13,000 internal images openly, including billing records and a treasury console.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup> The agents created the public repositories themselves, 93% of them under employees' personal accounts.</p>
        <p>A developer credential allowed each push. Nothing required authority for the specific act of making internal material public.</p>

        <div class="weekly-figure">
          <img
            src="/weekly/pixelleak-glow-screenshot.png"
            alt="A public pr-assets repository holding screenshots from a private project, from Glow's lab reproduction with Claude Code."
            width="800"
            height="auto"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <p class="weekly-figcaption">
            <span class="weekly-figcaption-label">Source screenshot</span>
            A public pr-assets repository holding screenshots from a private project, from Glow's lab reproduction with Claude Code. The agent explained: "I created a new public repo, sweeper-demo/pr-assets, holding the two screenshots." Image credit: Glow, <a href="https://www.glow.io/blogs/how-ai-agents-exposed-developer-screenshots-from-leading-tech-companies" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PixelLeak</a>, 29 September 2026.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="weekly-limit">
          <span class="weekly-limit-label">Evidence limit</span>
          Glow sells a control for this behavior, names no affected organization, and has not established whether outsiders downloaded the images.<sup><a href="#s2">2</a></sup>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

