    <!-- ── 04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact ──────────────────── -->
    <section class="weekly-section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="weekly-secnum">04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact</div>
        <h2>A benchmark tested approval bound to the exact bytes</h2>
        <p>A 28 September arXiv paper tested approval as "a one-shot credential bound to the exact bytes that will run."<sup><a href="#s15">15</a></sup> Attack success fell from 98.3% to 7.7%. The operating system sandbox, not the approval gate, produced the final drop from 30.8%.</p>

        <div class="weekly-figure">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 800 270" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of effective attack success rate by configuration: direct execution 98.3 percent, string gate 43.6, full architecture 30.8, plus file hash verification 30.8, deployed with OS sandbox 7.7.">
            <rect width="800" height="270" fill="#131318"/>
            <text x="20" y="28" fill="#6A7A88" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.2" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace">EFFECTIVE ATTACK SUCCESS RATE, % OF BENCHMARK ATTACK CASES</text>
            <g font-size="12" fill="#C9C3BC" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace">
              <text x="20" y="66">Direct execution (B0)</text>
              <text x="20" y="106">String gate (B2)</text>
              <text x="20" y="146">Full architecture (B5)</text>
              <text x="20" y="186">+ file hash check (B6)</text>
              <text x="20" y="226">Deployed, + OS sandbox (B7)</text>
            </g>
            <g>
              <rect x="230" y="52" width="484" height="20" fill="#363644"/>
              <rect x="230" y="92" width="215" height="20" fill="#363644"/>
              <rect x="230" y="132" width="152" height="20" fill="#363644"/>
              <rect x="230" y="172" width="152" height="20" fill="#363644"/>
              <rect x="230" y="212" width="38" height="20" fill="#D05010"/>
            </g>
            <g font-size="12" fill="#EEE8E0" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace">
              <text x="722" y="67">98.3</text>
              <text x="453" y="107">43.6</text>
              <text x="390" y="147">30.8</text>
              <text x="390" y="187">30.8</text>
              <text x="276" y="227" fill="#E86020">7.7</text>
            </g>
            <text x="20" y="258" fill="#6A7A88" font-size="10" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace">Source: Jing, arXiv 2609.38224, Table I. 313 case benchmark. Not production data.</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="weekly-figcaption">
            <span class="weekly-figcaption-label">Sourced chart</span>
            Values from Table I of the paper, 313 case benchmark. The deployed setup also denied 11.1% of benign tasks. Benchmark results, not production evidence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>An IETF individual draft published 29 September binds a permit to one recipient, one HTTP request, and one decided action, verified by the receiving system.<sup><a href="#s16">16</a></sup> It has no IETF standing.</p>

        <h3>Conditional assessment</h3>
        <p><strong>PixelLeak.</strong> An authority check could have stopped the public pushes if trigger logic classified visibility changes as consequential and the receiving system rejected any push lacking authority for that exact repository and file set. Coverage must include helper tools and personal accounts, where 93% of the images sat. The control stops publication, not the screenshot itself.</p>
        <p><strong>Manus.</strong> Authority for "check my inbox" does not cover running decoded code. A gate before execution would have held it. Salt's detection came after.</p>

        <p class="weekly-status">DAL-X status: the current demo runs end to end in a simulated enterprise environment. Production enforcement controls are still being completed.<sup><a href="#s17">17</a></sup></p>
      </div>
    </section>

